  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n0 0 5\n0 5 0\n5 0 0\nOutput:\n3 3 3\n4 4 4\n2 2 2\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 0 5\n0 0 5\n0 0 5\nOutput:\n3 3 3\n3 3 3\n3 3 3\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n5 0 0\n0 5 0\n5 0 0\nOutput:\n2 2 2\n4 4 4\n2 2 2\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n0 5 0\n0 0 5\n0 5 0\nOutput:\n4 4 4\n3 3 3\n4 4 4\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n0 0 5\n5 0 0\n0 5 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    # Find positions of 5s in the grid\n    positions = []\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        for c in range(len(grid[0])):\n            if grid[r][c] == 5:\n                positions.append((r, c))\n    \n    # Determine which rows contain 5s\n    rows_with_5 = set(r for r, c in positions)\n    \n    # Create output grid\n    output = []\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        if r in rows_with_5:\n            # Determine what value to fill based on column positions of 5s in this row\n            # Check which columns have 5s in this row\n            cols_in_row = [c for row, c in positions if row == r]\n            if 0 in cols_in_row:\n                # 5 is in left column -> fill with 2\n                output.append([2] * len(grid[0]))\n            elif 1 in cols_in_row:\n                # 5 is in middle column -> fill with 4\n                output.append([4] * len(grid[0]))\n            elif 2 in cols_in_row:\n                # 5 is in right column -> fill with 3\n                output.append([3] * len(grid[0]))\n        else:\n            # This shouldn't happen based on examples, but fill with 0 if no 5\n            output.append([0] * len(grid[0]))\n    \n    return output\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find positions of 5s in the grid\n    positions = []\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        for c in range(len(grid[0])):\n            if grid[r][c] == 5:\n                positions.append((r, c))\n    \n    # Determine which rows contain 5s\n    rows_with_5 = set(r for r, c in positions)\n    \n    # Create output grid\n    output = []\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        if r in rows_with_5:\n            # Determine what value to fill based on column positions of 5s in this row\n            # Check which columns have 5s in this row\n            cols_in_row = [c for row, c in positions if row == r]\n            if 0 in cols_in_row:\n                # 5 is in left column -> fill with 2\n                output.append([2] * len(grid[0]))\n            elif 1 in cols_in_row:\n                # 5 is in middle column -> fill with 4\n                output.append([4] * len(grid[0]))\n            elif 2 in cols_in_row:\n                # 5 is in right column -> fill with 3\n                output.append([3] * len(grid[0]))\n        else:\n            # This shouldn't happen based on examples, but fill with 0 if no 5\n            output.append([0] * len(grid[0]))\n    \n    return output",
